In article <5v3wkv@rpi.edu>, wilsonj@vccsouth07.its.rpi.edu (John Wilson) writes:
> The problem I've been having is getting the story straight on
> what license goes with what CPU -- DECdirect always says there
> are just two licenses, H and L;  H is for the 11/94 and 11/84
> (and corresponding 11/x3's?), L is for everything else.  Whenever
> I call and ask, whoever answers the phone thinks it works by the
> old deal, H is for Unibus and L is for Q-bus.  My machine is an
> 11/44.  Anyone have any recent first-hand experience on this?
> The difference is a couple weeks' pay, so I want to get the
> story straight before I start thinking that V04 isn't good enough
> for me...

  It's changed a few times (for a while the difference between H and L on
the 73/83 was whether you had PMI memory or not).

  At the present time, H applies to all Unibus systems and L applies to the
Q-bus systems. Since the only currently marketed systems are the 53/73/83/
84/93/94, both answers you received were correct.

  Almost all "old" systems were sold with the old "general" license, which
licensed you to use whatever operating system you first installed on the
machine.

  I suggest you locate the "DEC number" on the system (should be something
like 83012345N) and contact your local DEC sales office (real DEC, not a
distributor). Tell the salesdroid you want to purchase a "right to copy
update license" and "H-kit" for RT-11 on an 11/44. It is quite likely that
the salesdroid will happily take your order (especially if you do your home-
work and have the part numbers handy) without giving you the third degree
about existing licenses. This will save you some money over buying a whole
new license.

  [Obviously, this isn't DEC talking. This is me. Call your sales rep for
info on DEC's licensing policy du jour]

