Hmmmm.  I'm not sure if DEC uses higher tolerances for the manufacture of
TK70 media or not, but if you're getting away with it, it's fine.  Just be
ready for possible media dropouts if you're writing old CompacTape I (TK50)
tapes in a TK70.

The basic difference is that a TK50 holds 95MB and the TK70 handles 295MB.

Incidentally, you shouldn't need to use your magnet on a CompacTape II in
order to init it on a TK50 drive.  I do it all the time, and no special
treatment has ever been necessary.

Now, if you want some density, look at the T*85 series.  2.6GB on a DEC
CompacTape III cart, and soon to be 6.0GB!

