> Alpha does not allow unaligned memory references.

OK, I think it's clearing up. Since Alpha doesn't natively support unaligned
data, code designed for Alpha won't do unaligned references. However, the
low-order three bits of the address still have to be reserved to specify a 
byte address solely so that the architecture can detect and correct old
VAX code that did, right? This is why, even though the base unit of 
addressability on the Alpha is a quadword, the least significant three
bits (specifying longword, word, and byte) have not been completely dropped
from the architecture. Guess I'm a bit slow today...

Roger "Stark raving VAX" Ivie
