michael@cutler.com (Michael D. Shields) writes:
>We're interested in dropping our TK50 MDDS in favor of CDs.  Two
>nodes in our LAVc do not have local access to a CD reader (no HSC nor
>InfoServer, either).  We're concerned about VMS upgrades on these nodes.
>
>I believe that non-VMS distributions can be copied and VMSINSTALled from
>SYS$UPDATE.  Is the same true for VMS?  Somehow I doubt it.  Alternatively,
>can the appropriate save sets be copied prior to the upgrade from CDs to a
>TK50, thus building a TK50 distribution?  Is a TK50 VMS distribution just
>save sets?  Any boot information on the tape?
>
>So how is this done?
 
Typically speaking, the only installations you need to be concerned about are
major VMS upgrades.  All other distributions can be VMSINSTALled directly off
of the CD which you would have mounted cluster-wide.  More specifically
speaking, any software installation that requires the system to be rebooted
during the installation (as opposed to rebooting at the end of the installation)
requires that the distribution be on a local device.  Any other installation
can be done from a non-local device (MSCP served or via the network).
 
You needn't be concerned at all if the cpu(s) serving the LAVc's system
disk(s) also have a local CD reader.  If this is not the case, then you can
copy the distribution to the MFD (directory [000000]) of a disk that is local
and then do the install from there.
 
