In article <1992Jun25.123834.70@lrc.edu>, david@lrc.edu writes:
> Hello Folks:
> 
> I would like to know what the difference between a TK-50 and a TK-70 
> tape is.  I know that a TK-70 drive can read a TK-50 tape, but I can't 
> get a TK-70 drive to initialize a TK-50 tape.  Does anyone know if this
> can be done?  If so how?  Are the tapes different?  Thanks for any help.
> ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
> David J. Link                 david@alice.lrc.edu
> Lenoir-Rhyne College          
> Hickory, NC  28603 (USA)      I make no claims, but God does.

Someone else here mentioned that they were able to init a TK50 tape in a TK70
unit be degaussing it first.  That's true.  You can do that.  But first, a
little info on the differences between the two drives.

Some stats:  (As published in the Systems and Options catalog)

			TK50		TK70
			----		----
R/W Speed		75		100	inches/second streaming
Peak Xfer Total         62.5		125	Kbytes/second
Peak Xfer User Data	45		90	Kbytes/second
Number of Tracks	22		48
Recording method	both use a serial, serpentine pattern
Density			6667		10,000	bits/inch
Record Size		both are variable to (64Kbytes minus 1 byte)
Maximum Capacity	95		296	Mbytes formatted
Medium			both use a 600 ft 1/2 inch tape cartridge

Since a TK70 uses a larger number of tracks and a much higher density, the
recording medium is under more severe conditions.  A TK50 cartridge (aka
CompacTape Cartridge) has not been certified for use at the higher densities. 
You may be able to use it, but you may also find that you are more prone to
data loss.  You should be using a TK70 cartridge (aka CompacTape II Cartridge)
in your TK70 device.

Your question is a little vague though.  I'm not sure if what you are trying to
do is take a tape that has been used on a TK50 and re-init on a TK70 at TK70
density, or if you are trying to init a tape on a TK70 at TK50 density.

If you are putting a tape that contains information formated at the TK50
density into a TK70 device, the hardware forces a write lock condition. 
Meaning that it won't allow you to re-init the tape, even if you intend to
change the density.  The only solution is to degauss the tape and start fresh
as another poster mentioned.

If you are trying to init a tape in the TK70 to be read later on a TK50, that's
not possible.  A TK70 drive can read tapes formatted on a TK50 drive, but it
may not write at that density.  Your only option is to use a TK50 drive if you
wish to write a tape at TK50 density.  

And one other little caveat, a TK50 can not read a tape written on a TK70. 
I've never tried to re-init a TK70 density tape on a TK50 so I can't comment on
the feasability of that.  I'd imagine that it would work since the TK50 can't
read the tape in the first place, thus allowing to you init the tape at the
lower density.  Of course, whatever was on the tape will be lost.

Ken

