In article <1044@niktow.canisius.edu>, pavlov@niktow.canisius.edu (Greg Pavlov) writes:
> In article <1992Jun16.131316.23012@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>, marra@samuel.enet.dec.com (Dave Marra) writes:
>> 
>> Let me ask another question in response.  What makes going from a VAX to
>> an ALPHA running the same operating system any different than going from 
>> a DECstation / Ultrix to a Sparcstation/SUNos?  
>> 
>   I haven't had occasion to use VMS in some time.  But I think that the
>   more correct analogy in the above would have been "...any different than
>   going from a VAX /ULTRIX to a DECstation ULTRIX ?"  The major problem
>   we encountered in that arena was alignment.  The VAX was very "forgiving"
>   in this regard, the MIPS architecture wasn't/isn't.  I would assume the
>   same for the Alpha.
> 
>   greg pavlov
>   pavlov@fstrf.org
-- 
Greg,
 
Information presented in public forums, such as DECUS, indicates 
that VEST and the compilers will produce working code for data 
structures which are not aligned on their natural boundaries. 
Because of the need for multiple loads and masks, performance 
will be less than it COULD be, but the code will certainly 
execute.
 
In any event, even though it was hidden from the user, 
programmer, and compiler writer on the VAX processor, the 
efficiency penalty was just as bad. Each time an unaligned 
reference occurred, the processor had to perform one or more 
loads, masks, and shifts to bring the correct sized data item 
into its internal registers.
 
The Alpha architecture only makes the instructions required to 
perform this "housekeeping" visible, it does not introduce new 
CPU work which was not there originally.
 
