In article <1992Jun22.162540.29640@synapse.bms.com> (Stephen Jamieson) said
>
>We currently have a segment of our network built with Synoptics 3000 concentrators
>with a LAVC running over it. The topology of the net looks like this:
>
>                                HUB 3K
>                               / | | \
>                              /  | |  \    These are fiber optic between blgs.
>                             /   | |   \
>                            3K  3K 3K  3K  <--- nodes hang off here
>
>The LAVC is made up of two boot nodes, VAX4000s and 31 VAX3100 diskless nodes. The
>diskless nodes split evenly off the two boot nodes. 
>
>The problem is that the performance is awful on the LAVC. At first I thought this
>was solely related to the performance of the machines. But after using the
>Lattisnet managment software I became aware that the second-level concentrators
>were reporting high numbers of CRC and Misalignment errors. I thought the fiber
>might have been faulty so I switched fibers with no improvement. I noticed that when
>the LAVC nodes were booting, the CRC/Misalignment counts really shot up and the
>settled down after booting ceased. Checking the LAVC nodes counters in NCP shows
>high amounts of send failures, multiple collisions and single collisions. I isolated
>one of the concentrators and started replacing every piece of hardware but still 
>no change. I was wondering if there is are any problems with and LAVC over Synoptics ?
>My other Synoptics nets have tons of concentrators and much higher traffic but 
>don't have these high error counts or performance problems. Is there timing issues
>with LAVC that Synoptics can handle ? Any info would greatly be appreciated since
>I am near the end of my theory list. Thanks in advance.
>
--
Steve,
 
I have seen one or two strange problems on Synoptics networks 
before. At one client, we had a major problem, with the network 
(the Ethernet, that is) crashing several times a day at one 
point. 
 
The technicians from the dealer that had installed the equipment 
were not able to localize the problem, and my client was very 
unhappy (many activities were disrupted by the failures, which 
were occuring in a facility with several hundred people). 
 
The solution was in two parts: hardware and software. First, I 
isolated the network into more than one segment using local 
bridges, thus, if a concentrator problem was happening, we would 
be able to distinguish where the problem was. Second, I wrote a 
software tool to constantly monitor connectivity throughout the 
network. 
 
Fairly shortly, the network failed, but this time, the failure 
was limited to a specific section of one floor. We replaced the 
board in the concentrator for that section, and the problem was 
never seen again. 
 
The moral: It pays to put local bridges in conjunction with hubs 
and repeaters to prevent damage from spreading. 
 
- Bob
