>   I have simple questions to the RSXMC.MAC files, which were
>   generated by sysgen, when you install RSX-11M V4.2 on a 
>   PDP. Who can tell me something about the defined symbols and
>   the macros in such a file (an example is appended below) ?
>   Exists for this symbols a.s.o. good documents or books ? Is it 
>   possible to fit such a file for another PDP and which are the 
>   important symbols, because our FTP TCP/IP software, which
>   we want to install, needs such a file and the original file
>   was lost. The PDP, where we want to install this FTP-software
>   is a PDP 11/73, running RSX-11M PLUS V4.2 and has 2 x 800 MB,
>   2 x 1400 MB disks, 1 SONY Optical disk with 600 MB, Q-BUS,
>   and 1 MB of RAM.
>
>   What function have the macros after "exec macros" ?
>   Are they importat ? Please mail me your informations !
>
>   Thanks for your efforts,
>
>       Oliver
>
> .... Copy of RSXMC.MAC deleted ....
-- 
 
Oliver,
 
By way of introduction, I have over 15 years experience with RSX 
internals, and am the former RSX-11 Editor for Hardcopy magazine 
(which is no longer published). I have spoken on RSX internals 
in North America and overseas.
 
RSXMC.MAC contains a list of options and features selected to be 
included in the system. The file is used as an input to MACRO-11 
when assembling all of the components of the Executive, the 
device drivers, and most privileged code. Both the symbol 
definitions and the macros are used throughout the RSX-11M 
Executive, drivers, and tasks, so the entire file is needed.
 
The file can be considered to consist of three components, 
Executive configuration symbols, device configuration symbols, 
and general macros.
 
The first part, the Executive configuration symbols, can 
be recreated a variety of ways, depending upon which files you 
have and which files you do not have. For example, the values of 
some of the symbols can be ascertained by trying to use the 
Executive features that they control (e.g. If you are able to use 
LOGIN, and allocate devices, then your system must have symbol M$$MUP 
defined). Please note that most of the tests are .IFDF or .IFNDF, 
which means that the existence of the symbol is checked, not its 
value.
 
Other symbols can be similarly verified by examining parts of the 
Executive image. Another file which contains much of the 
information is file SYSSAVED.DAT, which is the saved answer file 
for the SYSGEN process. In the worst case, a new SYSGEN can be 
performed.
 
The device configuration macros define which devices are present 
in the system, and related parameters. They are used only in the 
assembly of the device drivers, so are not likely to be relevant 
in your case.
 
The remaining segment, the macros, are used throughout the Exec 
and all other privileged code. They are needed, but in any event, 
they are the same for all RSX-11M system, regardless of the 
configuration options selected (the macros themselves contain 
conditional text where appropriate).
 
I hope that the above is helpful. If I can be of further 
assistance, please do not hesitate to contact me.
 
- Bob
