In article <2018@tfd.tfd.com>, kent@tfd.COM (Kent Hauser) writes:
> 
> I need advice about how to set up a unattended file communications 
> system between a bunch of networked Suns (running SunOS 4.1) & HP boxes
> (running HPUX) and a remote VAX system (under VMS).
> 
> Several megabytes a day of logging data (used to generate customer bills)
> need to be transferred. These logging files will be transferred periodically
> (every n hours) from the unix boxes to the VAX.
> 
> These systems will be connected via a leased 56kbps link and TCP/IP.
> 
> Frankly, if there were just unix boxes on both ends, I would just set up
> a UUCP link (over TCP/IP). Some of the DEC types (in the data processing
> center) keep muttering the words FTAM, whatever that is. Price is
> basically no object in this endeavor.
> 
> My questions are:
> 
> 1) How's the best way to set this up?
> 2) What is FTAM?
> 
> 
> Thanks.
> 
> 	Kent
> -- 
> Kent Hauser			UUCP: {uunet,sundc,uupsi}!tfd!kent
> Twenty-First Designs		INET: kent@tfd.com
> (301) 572-6370
-- 
Kent,
 
First, the simplest answer. FTAM stands for "File Transfer, 
Access, and Management", which is the name of an international 
standard documented in ISO 8571 (ISO stands for International 
Standards Organization). Basically, FTAM is the international 
standard version of the functionality which is more or less 
provided by Digital's DAP protocol, which is used by DECnet 
through DECnet Phase IV. My recollection is that FTAM support is 
presently available for VMS as an add-on item, but I don't happen 
to have my DEC catalog handy, so I cannot give you the present 
name of the product. It will also, obviously, be part of 
DECnet-OSI.
 
The harder question is what to do at the moment. In none of your 
systems (HP, SUN, or DEC) would I consider OSI to be a mature 
software product. These products, when they are available, are 
new. There are several choices available to you. First, you can 
use the FTP protocol which should be part of your TCP/IP packages 
to transfer the files between machines. Alternatively, you could 
use UUCP running over TCP/IP to move the data. Either solution is 
useable, it depends upon which approach you favor. 
 
DECUS UUCP, which is available from the DECUS library, is quite 
reliable. It represents a reasonable option for this type of 
project.
 
I hope that the above is helpful.
 
- Bob
