In article <1992Jun27.174954.4099@athena.mit.edu>, jfc@athena.mit.edu (John F Carr) writes:
|>We run BSD on VAX 3100s at MIT, using Ultrix device drivers.
|>I recall the port taking more than two days.
|>
|>We've had problems with machines with more than 16 MB of memory:
|>
|>	The ethernet driver from ultrix 3.1 didn't work.

The LANCE chip (which is used in most DEC workstations) can only address
16MB of memory.  But if you allocate a large MBUF (2K), the two clusters
may not be in the same side of the 16MB boundary (if at all).  Only if
both clusters are below the 16MB, can you DMA into them.  Otherwise you
DMA into the local LANCE RAM and MOVC3 into the allocated mbufs.

|>	Sometimes a byte in the kernel text gets modified (always
|>	the same location).  This can cause a crash.

That's the footprint of the bug.
