No, due to some changes in Ultrix, close() doesn't return until the file has 
been flushed to disk. He measured speed to disk (but writing in 8KByte 
blocks, independent of the iozone block size parameter).

Ultrix's behaviour kills the performance of many of our programs (which 
tend to generate hundreds of files a minute). 
If we only could switch off this performance killing default ...

I reported that as a severe BUG (it is to us), but it seems DEC considers 
the file guaranteed system integrity more important than giving us a tool 
to get our work done ...

(Yes, I do prefer a system with deterministic behaviour. But I'd like to 
be able to select a asynch. close for those programs, which loose much 
possible performance now. Its even worse with some devices. 
Ultrix shows the same behaviour with EXABYTE drives, making them feel 
much slower under Ultrix than eg. under SunOS (not while streaming, 
but esp. on close).)

