Hello.  I'm looking for information on how people configure their
client/server networks for best utilization.  Here at Wellesley, we
have eight DECstation 5000s.  We currently have the entire Ultrix
distribution installed on one of the workstations which is acting as a
yellow pages and NFS server.  The other seven workstations have only
the base ultrix parts.  I have NFS mounted a hodgepodge of directories
in order to provide access to the software only installed on the server.

I am trying to convince my boss that this haphazard approach to
resorting to NFS mounting another directory whenever someone complains
they cannot run a specific program is too time consuming from a
management perspective and I feel we should use DMS to use our seven
clients as dataless workstations.  The biggest problem my boss has
with that is if the server is down, the clients are totally unusable.
He would like to see a solution where some sort of reduced
functionality would exist even if the server were down.  I feel that
the usability is so degraded when the server is unavailable that the
clients are practically unusable no matter how they are setup.


My question is - does anyone feel that having functionality when the
server is down is worth the time required to plan out exactly what
subsets should be installed on the clients, how to most easily move
from yellow page validated passwords to /etc/passwd validated
passwords, lack of availability of home directories, etc.  Does anyone
have any solution they feel is optimum?


