>Hello.  I'm looking for information on how people configure their
>client/server networks for best utilization.  Here at Wellesley, we
>have eight DECstation 5000s.  We currently have the entire Ultrix
>distribution installed on one of the workstations which is acting as a
>yellow pages and NFS server.  The other seven workstations have only
>the base ultrix parts.  I have NFS mounted a hodgepodge of directories
>in order to provide access to the software only installed on the server.
[...]

	Obviously, there's going to be no single approach that is "best"
overall, so it's hard to give you a straight answer for your question. In
general, the tradeoff you're dealing with is availability versus ease of
management and disk utilization.

	On the disk space and mangement side, if you put lots of stuff
on the server, you lose functionality when the server is down, but you
only have to manage it in one place, and save a lot of disk space. Some
of this can be offset by stuff like having two servers and using automount
and having both servers with the same software on them - but then you
have to deal with keeping stuff up to date (rdisting files back and
forth between /usr/local and whatnot).

	The approach I follow locally (which is dictated by how my group
works) is to have one central server that NFS serves /usr/local, which
contains a mess of stuff. It also serves CDROMS and sources and all the
things that everyone wants but nobody wants on their machine. Each system
on a desktop has its own "/home" with home directories, and its own root
and /usr with whatever stuff the user installs. Folks who don't want to
trust that the server will be up wind up wasting their local disk space
keeping multiple copies of applications so they can weather storms.

	Really, you need to base a configuration on *how your users work*.
If the requirement is that the environment look "the same from every
workstation" then I'd put root only on clients and share /usr and everything
else. (that way when the server is down, the clients still all look the
same - down.)  If the requirement is availability then you might want
to consider making a server a central repository for oft-used stuff,
but otherwise manage things locally. Some planning helps.

mjr.


