In article <13610001@hpspdla.spd.HP.COM>, stevewi@hpspdla.spd.HP.COM (Steve Witten) writes:
|>I am working on some software to filter packets captured on an
|>Ethernet and I have a couple of questions about DECnet Phase IV/V.

|>Does DECnet Phase V encapsulate its packets in IEEE 802.3 SNAP
|>frames?  How about Ethernet frames?

Depends.  DECnet Phase V is composed of many difference components, many of
which use the datalink directly.  DECnet Phase V uses the following Ethernet
protocols:

60-03		(Phase IV routing), 
60-02		(MOP V3 remote console/sysid/counters)
60-01		(MOP V3 downline load)
80-3C		(DECdns V1)

The following ISO 8802.2 Inidividual SAPs:

FE		(CLNS/ESIS/ISIS)
7E		(X.25 over LLC2)

And the following protocol ids (ie. SNAP SAPs): 

08-00-2B-60-01	(MOP V4 remote console/sysid/counters)
08-00-2B-60-02	(MOP V4 downline load)
08-00-2B-80-3C
08-00-2B-80-3E	(DECdts V1)

If you are using FDDI as a datalink, then the Ethernet protocol types above
well use ecapsulated Ethernet frames (OUI of 00-00-00) as per RFC 1188.

Note that new protocols (such as DECdts) and new major revisions (such as
MOP V4) use SNAP SAPs.  Also 08-00-2B is one of DEC OUIs (if you didn't know).
There is no 08-00-2B-60-03 since Phase IV is replaced by CLNS which has a
ISO assigned value for its ISO 8802.2 SAP.

|>Does DECnet Phase IV only use Ethernet frames?

Yes (but see FDDI comment above).

