>>
>DFS (Distributed File Service) allows you to mount a disk or directory
>remotely.

There is also a 3rd party package called Thruway from Software Partners that
does (effectively) the same thing. The big difference is that Thruway also
allows the remote access of tapes and print queues (although the remote printer
queue access is somewhat crude and outside the queue manager; if you want to
do remote printing over a DECnet WAN, go for DEC's DQS).

>The functionality is broadly similar to NFS, but uses a server with state, and
>supports most RMS semantics apart from shared write access.
>
>Works well over a WAN. I regularly access sites in Eastern USA using DFS.
>Accessing remote files this way give much better performance than using FAL,
>particularly for heavily hit servers and multi-file transfers.

We tested both products, and I agree that both offer significant performance
gains when compared to FAL access. Unfortunatly, we didn't do much in the
way of RMS attribute compliance, as we were mostly interested in tapes.

>The mount points are defined using DNS, so you need to have that working first.

One of the big advantates for us was that fact that Thruway did NOT require
a DNS license. DNS is fairly expensive, and we needed to support remote tape
access in 12 remote sites which would have been too costly.

Regards,

Dave.
