dittrich@milton.u.washington.edu (Dave Dittrich) writes:

>[...]  Why not just make a straight forward OSF/1 on MIPS
>AND Alpha?  None of this DEC "value added" crap, which takes you extra
>time and adds to the final cost.  Just make the first, best, and most
>stable OSF/1 platform around.

	This seems reasonable on the surface, and I myself used to hold
that view until I noticed how strongly some kinds of "added value" can
influence a purchaser's decisions. It's very easy to say that vendors
should all ship the same code (except for bus support and device drivers)
but are you really telling me that if Joe Bob Computer, Inc changes
their filesystem so it is 3X faster than everyone else's you won't
let that influence your buying decisions because they have done
"'value added' crap" to thier operating system? I doubt it.

	"Enhancing" (or breaking) existing code is a gamble. If you
make it much better you can win, and if you make it worse, you can
lose bigtime. As long as the "value added" doesn't break compatibility
with existing stuff IMHO it's purely the vendor's problem. They can
either gamble that they'll win because they ship faster, or that they
will ship later (because of "value added") and have a better product.

	I suspect there's a happy medium that every vendor needs to
find, between "just throw it over the wall" and "rewrite it/NIH". In
my ever so humble opinion it's something that most vendors do very
badly, because of shocking immaturity in software engineering. I
believe that certain "value added" operating system level optimizations
can be a huge win, but that indeed vendors should focus on value added
layered products that enrich the environment so much that they become
"must have"; such as NFS. That's where you make your money. If I
could make my filesystem 10X faster it would be worth 3 months of
my time because it'd make all our systems more competitive across
the board. That might be value worth adding. If I added a '-mumble'
option to "ls" so that it would so something silly like run its
output through the c preprocessor, it's not likely that's going to
make people run out and buy my machine, and I'm making a mistake.

mjr.


