Yes, the LPS-40 I have had experience with was also always breaking down. It
was a very finicky printer. It wanted to be on a hard-surface floor. It was
wihtout doubt, one of the messiest printers in terms of toner spills. And it
was noisy. On top of that, the print quality wasn't all that great.

Now, the LPS-20 was a _much_ nicer printer. With the duplexing option and the
large-capacity input tray, it was a real sweet piece of machinery. As for the
20ppm vs 40ppm argument, I'll add this: unless you're printing just text (ie,
no PostScript forms, etc), your thruput will depend to some extent on the
complexity of the PostScript you are shoving at the printer. In many instances
of sending un-optimised PostScript at the '40, it did not attain 20ppm print
rates. But the '20, with a slower print mechanism, would slow down to only
about 12 to 15 ppm (single-sided). When printing single-sided text, the '20
delivers a pretty consistent 18 to 20 ppm, day in, day out.

The print quality on the '20 was pretty nice, too. Much lower downtime.

dsa


