> Perhaps the following will clear up which direction Digital had in mind
> all the time.  After talking to someone in Digital about the Alpha, he
> commented that a system using an Alpha chip will always be twice as fast
> as a similar system using the same chip running OSF/1.  Is that
> possible?  Is the Alpha biased towards the VMS o/s?  If this is all true
> then it's not very fair, to say the least.  Perhaps he and/or I got it
> the wrong way around, or the difference is not that significant. 
> Anybody with some good information on this?
> 
> -- 
>   Jack N. Churchill                            | jack@syd.deg.csiro.au
-- 
Jack,
 
I can't answer this with numbers, because I have not run these 
particular tests. However, a general observation. Several years 
ago, we had a similar brouhaha over benchmarks in the workstation 
arena (I don't remember the case of characters in detail, so I 
will avoid all proper nouns [company names]).
 
In the end, it was noticed that even 2 processors running the 
same system (UNIX), with the same clock rates yielded different 
timings. A detailed examination showed several issues (not all 
present in each case)
 
     - compiler optimization settings
     - non-wait state memory/cache performance
     - disk interleaving
     - optimize/debugging mode
 
To put it bluntly, people were comparing apples (no pun intended) 
with oranges. It is not inconceivable that certain 
products/compilers/applications may run at significant speed 
spreads on different operating systems, even when the hardware 
platform is the same. 
 
For example, several years ago, we had an incident at one of the 
sites I was involved in. The system (a 11/780) seemed to stop 
doing productive work. When we tracked down the guilty program, 
it was a plotting program (written for UNIX) which was trying 
(and succeeding, so to speak) to issue single character QIOs to 
the plotter at 9.6kbps. Needless to say, the resulting 
performance was horrific. The users answer was, "It seems to work 
fine under UNIX". In point, he had just blithely gone and done 
this, without considering whether his translation of the UNIX 
system calls were appropriate (it wasn't). A small change 
dramatically improved performance (and reduced CPU load).
 
These incidents occur continously. It is too simple (generally 
out of lack of experience) to produce what appears to be a simple 
benchmark, only to discover that it contains severe hidden 
biases.
 
My apologies for the length of this posting. I expect that the 
base speeds will be very close, at least when the systems 
requests are at a minimum and the compilers are the same!
 
- Bob
