There's a couple of replies in this string (Jim Gettys of Digital, Bob Gezelter 
and Reha Elci) that I'll try to respond to at once.

First off, like Jim said, much of what is being speculated about in this
newsgroup is proprietary, and thus I can't give definitive answers.  
However, as Bob  said, I delivered a presentation on the alpha workstations
at DECUS (available in PS format for those who send me mail and I believe
that Gail has put it somewhere for copying but don't know where it is.  Gail?)
that described much of the internals of the CPU design and such.


Now, to the questions:


The initial Alpha workstations will NOT use the same memory as the DECstation
or any other platform that we've done in the past for a variety of reasons
including performance, width and lack of ECC protection.  As Bob said, 
we address the memories in 256-bit chunks for performance, and obviously using
16-bit or 8-bit wide industry standard SIMMS wouldn't cut it for that 
design.  

However, I would suggest you talk to your DEC sales person if you're 
considering buying right now.  There are plans in place to take care of you
if you buy a DECstation (or VAXstation for others interested) as well as your
large memory configurations.  I can't say more about the programs in a public
place like this.


As for the other peripherals, keyboards, mice and such.  We have 
said in public that the Alpha workstations will have SCSI and TURBOchannel
(by the way, that's the trademarked spelling if you're interested) support.

The SCSI is DEC's SCSI and we're qualifying all of the RZ and TZ devices that
we support today on the VAXstation and DECstation line.  Third parties will
of course have the same restrictions that they do today, and a list of SCSI 
commands necessary will of course be published as they are with today's 
products.

The TURBOchannel is the same EXACT TURBOchannel used in the DECstation
5000/2xx series.  We couldn't change this bus and kill the work that a large
number of TRI/ADD third parties have done in getting their products
onto the TURBOchannel, it wouldn't make sense.  Add to that, we're not 
in the business or re-inventing lots of things like TURBOchannel FDDI, graphcs
and Ethernet controllers so we've got a vested interest in it being the 
same so our boards work.  The end result is that boards from the DECstation
family are compatible with the hardware.  Now, software drivers (especially
for third parties) and availability is another question that I don't have
answers for right now.


Finally, I don't want to people to mis-understand what has been said here.
Upgrades from DECstation 5000's to desktop/deskside Alpha workstations is
NOT  a board swap.  There just isn't enough air movement or power in 
the DECstation boxes to make this possible.  The upgrades will be a box-swap
upgrade




--


