>Yes, they are different animals.  But the operating system is the same.  
>Why wouldn't it be a matter of recompiling everything?  Don't you write 
>portable code? ;*)  
Actually, yes I do.  But my experience has been that most of the code I have
seen written for VAX/VMS isn't.  The manuals are often circumvented in favor
of using some undocumented behavior that works.
>
>Let me ask another question in response.  What makes going from a VAX to
>an ALPHA running the same operating system any different than going from 
>a DECstation / Ultrix to a Sparcstation/SUNos?  
The biggest difference I see is that UNIX programmers expect differences, and
so code is more often written to be easy to port.  VAX/VMS programmers did
not expect a radical change in architecture, and so the code is far less
portable on average.

>So, I believe it is an order of magnitude easier to port from VAX/VMS
>to Alpha/VMS because the operating systems are the same.
Again, my point was that many people seem to think it will be no effort at
all.  That is certainly not true.
