In two articles with subject:
>Re: What is the most recent release of DECwindows/MOTIF for Ultrix?

Tonnie Geraets (tnnrtg@ctrl.phys.tue.nl) asked:

>>But don't you need Motif compliant applications (including DECterm,
>>Session Manager, etc.) to obtain a 'consistent screen appearance and
>>behaviour'?

And Reha Elci (reha@cunixf.cc.columbia.edu) replied:

>yes but what is the problem? All dx* applications are already converted
>to motif in 1.1.3.  I use decterms, calendar, cardfiler, dxmail,
>session mgr, dxnotepad etc etc. they are all using motif widgets now.
>the base system still comes with dxterm etc done with dwt but when you
>load 1.1.3, it upgrades the whole environment to motif. they've done a
>nice job.

Do these Motif compliant dx* applications use the MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1
authorization scheme?

As already has been mentioned in these newsgroups, the DECwindows
applications in the base system do NOT support that authorization
scheme.  Authorization per user is available in X11 release 4 and
release 5 (XDMCP protocol), but is NOT supported in the DECwindows
clients (dx*).  Moreover, although DECs X server (Xws) has options to
enable XDMCP, the use of xdm is NOT supported by Digital.

This is unsecure and even dangerous, and we have formally asked DEC to
support XDMCP in the DECwindows environment.

The answer we got was: certainly NO XDMCP support in Ultrix 4.2c (very
soon now?), NOT in Ultrix 4.3 (4Q92?), perhaps in Ultrix 4.4 ...

In the meantime we have decided for security reasons to abandon the use
of DECwindows.

What we would like to know is: when converting the dx* applications in
the Motif 1.1.3 release to use Motif widgets, were these applications
also brought to X11 release 4 level?  In other words: do they support
the use of the MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 authorization scheme?

