>Is it a way to use Unix commands with VMS? 

	POSIX is a "Portable Operating Systems Interface" - basically
it's a distillation of UNIX system calls and commands to a lowest
common denominator, that application developers can code to, and can
rely on support for in any POSIX system. POSIX is really a suite of
standards (some still in progress) - the first two are system calls
and services, and commands. The system call standard defines a UNIX
like API for opening files and devices and whatnot. The commands
standard specifies standard behaviors for common or garden shell
commands such as "ls".

	VMS' POSIX implementation includes system calls and shell
commands, so in theory at least, you can get on a VMS machine that
is running the POSIX 1003.2 suite, and "grep" things. ;) Heck, if
it runs "grep", it's UNIX, right?  ;)

mjr.
