I suspect the problem isn't with null pointer constants, but with pointers
containing a zero value. If you dereference such a pointer (and thus access
location zero) the standard doesn't say what will happen. On a VMS VAX you'll
get an memory access violation, because the first 512 bytes are intentionally
inaccessible to catch this sort of error/laziness. On an IBM PC nothing much
will happen unless you're using the large memory model and writing to
location 0, in that case you'll trash your hard disk.

I don't know about VAX/Ultrix & Alpha/whatever, but I'd hope that location 0
would be inaccessible, the VMS method is very useful for trapping programming
mistakes with pointers.

Steve


