In <1992May22.203350.2857@ninja.zso.dec.com> cja@chmist.zso.dec.com (Carl J. Appellof) writes:

>>Can
>>it be used to back up NFS-mounted disks which are on a VAX/VMS system? 

>Yes, but you really have to force it.  DECnsr doesn't normally backup
>NFS-mounted file systems.  The view is that it's better to backup these
>files by running the client software on the machine where the files
>actually reside.

There is a lot of wisdom in this strategy.

A system manager must consider bandwidth.  Do some
arithmetic on NFS/ethernet to tape vs. Local disk to tape.
I find my little system (Indigo) takes way too long to
backup over the net to a remote tape.  It is cost effective
to backup locally.  Worse I do not do it often enough.

Following the bandwidth note.  One of todays grand
challenges is to provide a set of tools and views of machine
collections that is user friendly and seamless.  When I
first started playing with 4 and 6 MHz 68000 processors a
10Mbit ethernet had more bandwidth than most disk systems.
This is no longer true.

Today we have engines that can gobble instructions and data
at massive rates.  At one level our new Crimson (R4000)
machines have and use a memory bandwidth of 300 Mega Bytes
per second.  Users that execute with binaries or data on an
NFS resource will be strangled.  Our worthy competitors have
or will be building systems of this class as well.

I suspect system managers will need to think very hard about
their network environment.  The balance between what the
system manager needs and the user community wants.  This
could be a problem when such a thin bandwidth thread
interconnects a collection of systems must be shared.

An additional challenge is the venerable old work horses
stable software, paid for, running applications correctly off
in a room somewhere.  These machines have been used to a
moderately loaded net.  How well will they behave in the
presence of the new generation of systems?  An Indigo was 
reported by one university to be using 70% of a network
for NFS.  At this site -- this was unheard of.  Since he
had +500 systems on this one network he had a challenge.

--

