The quickest way for you to try out double buffering is to set the
following environment variable before running your program:

	PHIGSdouble_buffering True

	Next thing you might want to experiment with, do you want
	to do "pixmap" double buffering (where PHIGS draws to
	a pixmap, then does a copy_area to a window on screen) or
	do you want to use MIT double buffering extension?  To
	use MIT double buffering, again, an environment variable:

	PHIGSmit_db_enable True

	For control within your program, you should look at
	the escapes section of the doc set (I believe that's
	volume 5, Appendix A) *AND* the appropriate device
	specifics (PEX).

	If you installed PHIGS examples when you installed PHIGS,
	take a look at phigs_xdemo.c for further hints.

	Finally, if you should wish to venture into the world
	of immediate mode PHIGS, check out the release notes for
	some additional escapes to give you more control over
	double buffer swap behaviour.

