Without wanting to argue about the overall point (I didn't try to migrate
anything from Solaris 1.0 to 2.0, the only life experience is with
Alpha), let me correct one misconception.

In article <BpBtwn.1E4@accelr8.com> jim@accelr8.com (Jim Reiss) writes:
>>
>What makes you say that?  VMS currently lets you get away with any sort of
>data alignment you like, RISC processors tend to choke (especially RISC
>processors without byte and word load/store instructions, which I believe
>Alpha's is).

The lack or presence of byte/halfword instructions does have nothing
to do with data alignment problems. Note the SUN Sparc, which *has*
the former and bombs on the latter. It is up to *compilers* to
implement unaligned access code sequences and I believe the SUN
compilers do this for the Sparc if asked to (using byte accesses and
shift/or) and the Alpha/VMS compilers do this (using load/store_unaligned
and the in-register zap/mask/extract instructions to do this). Neither
Sparc nor Alpha is as spiffy about this as the MIPS lwl/lwr sequences,
but other than the performance impact, there is nothing that
keeps you from using unaligned accesses in Alpha. They are *discouraged*
for performance reasons, but that's about it.

