In article <3335@ais.com>, arthur@ais.com writes...
>I believe that RSX-11D had shared libraries from the beginning.  They
>also swapped in and out as needed by other tasks.  (BTW this was the
>BIG problem with getting the bugs out of the swapper/time-slicer -- 
>making sure all the shared regions were properly loaded.)
> 
>RSX-11M also had shared libraries from its beginning some years later.

	Actually RSX-11D started out with two different functions:

	A/ Shared code. Any program such as an editor could be linked with
	   separate read-only (ie code) segments. Thus you gained considerably
	   in total core usage when 2 or more people were doing edits, compiles
	   etc. This only came around about version 6 circa '76 or so. (Be
	   fore that you couldn't have multiple versions of the same task, so
	   we used to run PIP, POP and PUP, EDI, EDO and EDU, etc.)

	B/ Shared global areas, which could be a library of subroutines or
	   a global data area. This was around since (swag,) version 4 or
	   so, circa 1972.
