In article <1992Jun11.094025.1991@lsl.co.uk> robin@lsl.co.uk (Robin Fairbairns) writes:
>> What makes you say that?  VMS currently lets you get away with any sort of
>> data alignment you like,
>
>VMS is not a processor.  The correct statement is "VAX processors absorb 
>the cost of non-aligned data in the hardware, so that you see slowed 
>execution of single instructions, rather than having to write out lots 
>of alignment instructions yourself".
No, I meant VMS.  And everything which goes with it, including the compilers.
I have never heard of an "alignment instruction".  Code can either have
aligned or non-aligned data, and if you're calling one function which expects
aligned structures and one which expects non-aligned, you're in trouble.
VMS was designed with the assumption that data is not aligned, because no
one ever expected to divorce it from its supporting processor.  Alignment
was just an example, in any case.  I have seen a lot of VAX/VMS code ported
to RISC systems, and the code has often made assumptions about an underlying
architecture which will not necessarily exist on Alpha.
>
>>               System calls also tend to accept 16 and 32 bit parameters,
>> which DEC can support for now but will need to change at some point to get
>> the most out of a 64 bit processor (which their competitors will force them
>> to do).  There are a lot of CISC versus RISC issues which DEC will have to
>> deal with in some way, and I am sure DEC will have to choose the RISC way
>> in some cases, ease-of-porting be damned.
>
>From what I've seen of the migration path, I'm impressed.  Porting seems 
>to be a whole lot less hassle than I'd expected.  Which is not to say 
>it'll be easy ;-)
I'm sure they are making every effort to make it easier for people.  I just
can't believe that it is as painless as DEC claims it will be.  I found it
particularly telling that DEC was proposing a six-month period where Alpha
customers will also get a VAX.  If they're basically the same but one is
faster, what do you need the VAX for?
