In article <1992Jun12.144950.114@degsyd.syd.deg.csiro.au> ip_boss@degsyd.syd.deg.csiro.au (Jack Churchill) writes:
>In article <1808@ltb.ltb.bso.nl>, wierda@ltb.ltb.bso.nl (Gerben Wierda) writes:
>> Digital has made a reassuring statement on OSF/1 on MIPS. Does that mean that
>> we are out of the woods. I think we are not.
I agree.  We've climbed out of the fire and back into the frying pan.

>> 
>> 1. Digital is a company that has some pretty serious problems. There main
>> problem is that they have too many employees. They have the lowest sales per
>> employee of all large companies. Their first neighbour on the list has twice
>> as much sales per employee. Digital will remain in a state where they will
>> have to look for ways to cut costs for quite a while yet.
>> 
>> 2. Having so many hardware platforms and so many OS's to support costs too
>> much.
>> 
>> 3. There have been serious investments in VMS ("open VMS" etc) and in Alpha.
>> 
>> So for now the near-revolt of the customers has made them publish a reassuring
>> statement. From a PR point of view they couldn't have done otherwise. As long
>> as the products they want to focus on (Alpha) are not ready they will have to
>> protect at least current sales.
>> 
>> As amateur analist of this market I would not trust this statement. Not
>> because Digital is a company of lying people, but just because they are in
>> such a bad situation that this commitment has a high probability of breaking
>> under the financial strain. Especially since the company has been at war with
>> itself for years and years on the OS issue.
>

1.  Digital does not *necessarily* have "too many employees."

Wall Street analysts called for the latest round of downsizing.  In my
opinion DEC management would have preferred to quit laying off last
year, but their profits would not allow this.  At about 10:00
yesterday there was a composite quote of below 39 for Digital stock
(this is public information).  It is unfortunate that investors don't
see the potential in DEC's future, but that really isn't my business.
Maybe it is a PR problem.  DEC has been adding to thier payroll lately
by purchasing profitable companies (ie.  800-SOFTWARE).  In addition
the layoffs so far have been mostly sales and marketting, if there
have been engineers layed off they have been part of sales support and
marketting support, not product development.

If Digital does have a problem it is that: a) they can't seem to
convince customers that they will support the existing product lines
via upgrades and continued support and b) they can't seem to convince
investors that thier plans for the future are stable.  If they can fix
problem 'a' DEC can earn enough profits to meet thier admittedly large
payroll.  If they can fix problem 'b' then they have the confidence in
thier structure to avoid being sued for mismanagement by stockholders
until Alpha can be delivered.  These layoffs are penance for DEC's
percieved failures in these areas.

2.  Having lots of products does not *necessarily* "cost too much."

Digital is a hardware company.  The production of OS and Application
Software at DEC is mainly geared toward selling more boxes.  Having
too many products only costs too much if you can't sell enough
hardware to justify it.  This justification is a tremendously complex
task.  Especially with VMS (which definitely justifies its existence),
Ultrix (which could be argued both ways as justifiable and not), and
NT (how can a product that is not selling yet be justified).  Digital
has never really been able to justify any OS based on the cost of
developing it.  As for justifying a variety of hardware, it's just
like building cars or washing machines -- if you can sell 'em for a
profit, then build 'em.

3.  Alpha, an architecture after God's own heart.

Okay maybe not, but Alpha does fix alot of DEC's problems.  DEC can
cut the cost of supporting so many different OS's by consolidating
them to a single CPU.  This is why they have emphasized the Privileged
Software Library feature.  They can fix the questionable profitability of
supporting a U**X platform by becomming the primary manufacturer of
the hardware instead of the middleman.  They can sell more hardware by
offerring a VMS platform which is at least competitive with the
competition.  If NT really does take the place of windows, DEC can
again become the primary provider of PCs by offering Alpha+Windows as
an alternative to Intel architectures.

>That pretty much agrees with what I've been saying for a long time.  I
>also suggested that Digital should "freeze" VMS where it is since it's
>already a mature and stable o/s, and put most of its energy into OSF/1. 
>Perhaps Digital can do both but how well?  It runs the risk that other
>companies will provide better systems and tools much sooner.  It's time
>Digital and it's VMS customers realize that some things do have to
>change (not just for the sake of it though).  After all I didn't
>complain when we went from RSX to VMS many years ago.  How about we now
>go from VMS to OSF/1+{VMS}?
>

Right, orphaning VMS would cause traditional VAX customers to consider
other vendors along with DEC and possibly hurt the sale of hardware.
That would be the reverse of DEC's historic position of using the OS
to sell the hardware.  It isn't out of the question but why should DEC
level a playing field that is slanted in thier direction.

The assumption throughout this discussion has been that OS development
is a big ticket item at DEC and that cost cutting will necessarily hit
this part of the company.  The current pattern of resource management
and what we have seen of DEC's future doesn't really point in that
direction.  There will have to be alot of Sales and Marketting people
out of the way before DEC starts hitting up software development and
thier will have to be alot of application developers out of the way
before DEC starts hitting OS development.  OSF/1 on Alpha fit's into
DEC's philosophy alot better by replacing the costly development and
support of Ultrix with one developed by another organization and
moving from low margin MIPS to high margin Alpha.

To convince customers to buy machines now, DEC has to convince them
that upgrades won't break the bank and that they will not be required
immediately (ie. the OSF/1 fiasco).  To convince investors that the
stock is worth owning they must a) cut costs, but more importantly b)
show some sense of purpose in thier management.  How many of us are
really waiting on pins and needles for an Ultrix upgrade that doesn't
even support shared libraries.  How many of us couldn't have waited
awhile for 800-SOFTWARE to become part of Digital.  How does this
purchase compare with DEC's blowing off Apple (are PC's important or
aren't they!).  As customers we have the obligation to do the smart
thing: a) send them the message by asking for guarantees on price and
availability on upgrades, and b) judge them by what they do and not
what they say (a difficult task given DEC's corporate schizophrenia).

-- Les Walker

I am not an employee of Digital Equipment Corporation.  These views
are mine alone and do not reflect those of my employer in any way.


