A number of us here are now the proud keepers of brand spanking new
DECstation 5000/25's.  Included in the package is this 3.5" RX26 floppy
drive.  

The floppy drive maintenance utility program (fddisk, which by the way
is *not* findable vie apropos(1), since the whatis database is shipped
obsolete) has a formatting option.  This leads to a few questions.

* There is a parameter ``-i<nnn>[:<ccc>]'' which the Usage text describes
  as:
  
    -i<nnn>[:<ccc>  Use interleave factor <nnn> for format.
	  If specified, <ccc> indicates additional
          inter-cylinder interleave.

  Now, I'm familiar with sector interleave (interleave 2 means you write
  every second sector, requiring two revolutions to write an entire track),
  and the reasoning for using this.   It's the "cylinder interleave"
  that leaves me puzzled.   What is it?  Why is it?  Where is it documented?
  I looked in the obvious manuals, and found nothing.

* The interleave defaults to ``-i2:4''. What are the performance implications
  of changing the sector interleave?  The cylinder interleave?  Are there
  _other_ implications we should be aware of?

* Reading through the include files for the fd driver, I see hints about
  undocumented formatting capabilities in the fddisk utility.  Specifically,
  I see hints that there is (or will) exist the ability to lay down either 
  an MSDOS filesystem or an ULTRIX filesystem on an RX26 disk.  Applying
  a little empiricism, I find that this actually *works*!  (Or seems to,
  at least.)  

  So, what's the word on this "feature"?

* It is possible to "convert" a 1.44 MB disk to a 2.88 MB disk by punching
  the appropriate "ID slot" in the floppy carrier.  Does anyone have any
  feel for how well 1.44 media will retain data at 2.88? 
  
  I have this suspicion (based on nothing more than "gut feel" and a
  remembrance of media (non)changes in the 1600 to 6250 transition in
  10" round-tape) that the media itself is good for densities a lot higher
  than are currently being used.  It's just that there is, as yet, little
  demand for the higher densities, thus the media manufacturers don't
  bother to certify media at densities higher than they currently market.

  What this all boils down to is that I have this "feeling" that the
  HD (1.44MB) type disks will be quite usable at ED (2.88MB) densities,
  and are probably much less expensive(*).  Anyone want to hit me with the
  technical details on why I shouldn't be doing this?

  [* NOTE: I'm assuming media from reputable manufacturers, not the 
    "super cheap" suppliers.  I'm also keeping in mind DEC's excursion
    into the Rainbow market, with *it's* "quad-density" floppies which
    were initially ONLY available from DEC, pre-formatted.  Turns out
    that the standard off-the-shelf DD 5" diskettes were perfectly suitable
    for the Rainbow -- once you had a program to format them :-).  I'm
    suspicious that a similar situation holds in the 3.5" RX26 media
    arena -- except that in this case DEC _does_ supply a formatter
    program.]


I await the deluge of replies.  :-)
