In article <1992Jun8.003503.2446@uwm.edu>, jgd@csd4.csd.uwm.edu (John G Dobnick) writes:
> 
>   Now, I'm familiar with sector interleave (interleave 2 means you write
>   every second sector, requiring two revolutions to write an entire track),
>   and the reasoning for using this.   It's the "cylinder interleave"
>   that leaves me puzzled.   What is it?  Why is it?  

Cylinder interleave (aka cylinder skew) changes the starting position of the
beginning of each cylinder. Normally, sector 1 is immediately after the index
hole. Cylinder skew will place sector 1 there on track 0, but will move it
to another position on track 1. The point is to cover for the seek time of the
drive; if you step from track to track, it takes time for the drive to step
and the cylinder skew is an attempt to make certain that the first sector of
the next track is available immediately following the step time from the last
sector of this track.
> 
> * The interleave defaults to ``-i2:4''. What are the performance implications
>   of changing the sector interleave?  The cylinder interleave?  Are there
>   _other_ implications we should be aware of?
> 
It really depends on what the driver is doing. I have no idea what the Ultrix
driver does, so I can't say for sure. The floppy controller is designed to
handle 1:1 interleave; you can request a read of the entrire floppy with a
single SCSI command, the controller will buffer up 32KB at a time and ship
it out over the SCSI bus. The RX26 model even does synchronous transfers.

This means it should be a really hot floppy for RT-11 people.

> 
> * It is possible to "convert" a 1.44 MB disk to a 2.88 MB disk by punching
>   the appropriate "ID slot" in the floppy carrier.  Does anyone have any
>   feel for how well 1.44 media will retain data at 2.88? 

Actually, the media are radically different between 1.44MB and 2.88MB. The
1.44MB cannot sustain 2.88MB data rates on the inner tracks. It can do them
just fine on the outer tracks (I once even formatted a 720KB floppy that
was 1/3 2.88MB, 1/3 1.44MB, and 1/3 720KB). The inner tracks are the worst
case because they're the shortest. Note that the RX26 controller can't read
a floppy with a wierd format like the one I described, but if you've got a
CP/M box with a 2.88MB-capable controller on it...

>   [* NOTE: I'm assuming media from reputable manufacturers, not the 
>     "super cheap" suppliers.  I'm also keeping in mind DEC's excursion
>     into the Rainbow market, with *it's* "quad-density" floppies which
>     were initially ONLY available from DEC, pre-formatted.  Turns out
>     that the standard off-the-shelf DD 5" diskettes were perfectly suitable
>     for the Rainbow -- once you had a program to format them :-).  I'm
>     suspicious that a similar situation holds in the 3.5" RX26 media
>     arena -- except that in this case DEC _does_ supply a formatter
>     program.]

The Rainbow was a special case in that the only difference between Rainbow
media and normal DSDD media is the number of tracks. The Rainbow uses the
same data rate, which controls the type of medium used. Doubling the number
of tracks on a floppy and doubling the data rate are two different
things.

Roger Ivie
ivie@cc.usu.edu


