It has been "interesting" to watch the discussions about Digital
providing OSF/1 on our existing MIPS-based investments deteriorating
into a religious VMS vs. UNIX war.  In fact, the noise level over
whether or not VMS is more "open" than UNIX has reached such a level,
there there is little or no discussion on the original, CRITICAL issue.

There are some who for years have wished that UNIX would just go away.
I remember at the Atlanta symposium a few years ago, coming out of the
VIP (VMS Integrated Posix) announcement, hearing more than one person
commenting that "now there is no reason to use UNIX anymore".
Obviously, that is not the case, because regardless of how "open" VMS
may or may not be, it is still not as "portable" to other architectures
as UNIX is.

The original issue, was to tell DEC whether we wanted OSF/1 on our
existing MIPS-based, Digital hardware investments.  The arguing over the
definitions of "open", the "VMS is better than UNIX" and "UNIX is better
than VMS" arguments are NOT getting us any closer to our goal.  Quite
the contrary, it could be used by "some" as proof that the population of
Usenet and these groups in particular, have no great desire to assist
Digital in its decision process, and all the "information" that has been
provided through these channels is NOT something to be taken seriously.

So, can we PLEASE get back on track?  Can we pour some cool logic on the
flames of a religious OS war?  We need to help Digital make a decision.
To do that, we need to show them that we are not drooling, flaming
operating system bigots.  We need to show them that what we want is in
Digital's best interests.

I want OSF/1 on my existing hardware investment.  I will vote with my
budget.

-- 
                Kurt Reisler (UNISIG Chair, DECUS US Chapter)
