Well it wasn't as quick as I'd hoped, for two reasons.  First, the replies
kept dribbling in and I didn't put an upper bound on how long I'd collect
them; second, not a single person kept to the format of my "quick survey".
Every respondant sent a free-form essay, and I've been running in circles
trying to figure out how to "tabulate" the results.

Predictably, I'm taking the easy way out and including the entire set of
results below.  I have edited the comments for form (removing line wraps,
adding paragraph breaks, and so on) but not for content: everything, even
flames of the form "you guys at DEC are all idiots" has been reproduced
faithfully.  (You'll have to take my word for that, I guess.)

I do have some editorial comments, though.  After I posted my survey, three
different newspaper reporters contacted me.  What a shock: people in the
real world are reading Usenet, and are asking for pithy quotes for front-
page articles.  I declined all requests, and I will decline all future
requests.  Our PR person is named Dave Price and you can reach him by phone
at +1 603 884 3467 or via electronic mail at <Dave.Price@TTB.MTS.DEC.COM>.

Nothing that I say, here or elsewhere, is ever the official position of
Digital Equipment Corporation.  You may NOT quote me in any publication
other than Usenet, unless you have my express written permission, which you
will never, ever, ever get.  You will find this disclaimer to be more or
less true for all Digits who post articles to Usenet.  Digital is very
relaxed about what we say publically as long as we don't tell you anything
that hasn't already been made public through Dave Price's organization and
as long as we never give permission to be quoted.

Now that we've got that out of the way, let's read what folks had to say
about Digital's Ultrix/MIPS/OSF1 strategy.  Note that the rumour that was
rampant at the time of my survey was that Digital was not going to support
OSF/1 in "production" on the MIPS platforms.  It was only a rumour; I know
from direct personal observation of the parties involved internally that
no decision had been made at the time these rumours surfaced.

The essay-answers to my survey follow.  I notice that the quality of DEC
Field Service seems to vary a great deal from customer to customer... I am
going to make sure that Gail Grant and Fred Avolio take this message and
all the messages appended below, and push it as far up the chain as it will
go.  I'm also going to post it on an internal Digital "Notesfile" that gets
a lot of traffic by Open Systems sales folks.  And without telling any
secrets I want to let you all know that your words, both on the newsgroup
and as sent to me for this survey, _have_ made a difference internally.

