We bought 50 DECstation 3100s plus associated servers (3 x 5400) for
teaching purposes at the end of 1989. There was fierce competition between
DEC, Sun and HP for the order. It was understood by all that the investment
was supposed to last five years (which is why we turned down VAX or 68000
solutions and went RISC, stretching the budget to do so).

The lack of shared libraries in Ultrix was a serious concern, but we were
assured that they would come "Real Soon Now". But we felt that we were
buying an overpowered system, and that shared libraries, when they came,
would effectively act as a disc and memory upgrade when we needed it.  In
fact, the system as a whole performed abysmally, and is still only just
acceptable even though we had to spend a substantial amount of extra money
which we could ill afford on a real disc and memory upgrade only a few
months after the initial purchase.

To be told at this stage that we won't ever get shared libraries on these
machines is just a further kick in the teeth. Of course, the salesman who
made the original promises has long since moved on.

No, the machines aren't going to be scrap metal. We HAVE to use them for at
least two more academic years because there is no money to replace them.
Meanwhile we have to struggle on, explaining to generation after generation
of Computer Science students why their programs take forever to link and
why they can only build one or two X programs before they run out of disc
quota.  What sort of impression of Digital are they going to take with them
into the real world?

