Subject: Re: "DEC plans to bypass OSF/1 on MIPS systems"

In article <VIXIE.92May20180618@cognition.pa.dec.com>, you write:

> how many of you folks own vaxes or decstations that
> you are going to sell as scrap metal if digital doesn't give you shared
> libraries and ongoing operating system development?  

Sell as scrap metal?  Not likely.  (Actually, I kind of like Ultrix because
of its stability.  I dread the arrival of a completely new O.S. because
even if it promises great new features, it always breaks lots of existing
code, and requires more resources than the old O.S..)

Shared libraries and fixes to major bugs are welcome, of course, but other
extensions probably aren't necessary.  We probably don't care about OSF/1
until we can get it on all of our platforms and use its distributed file
system instead of NFS.  However, at that point, anything that doesn't
support the OSF/1 DFS (or whatever we end up using) will be an orphan.

[I've considered implementing shared libraries myself using gcc, gld, and
the sysv-shared memory extensions.  Either that or by writing a character
device driver just to hold shared libraries so I can use mmap().  Not
pretty, but it might work well enough.]

> how many of you would keep your vaxes and decstations if 4.4bsd supports 
> them, but will sell them and buy SPARC machines if 4.4bsd supports SPARC 
> but not vaxes or decstations?

We will probably keep them in either case.  On the other hand, there is
already some reluctance to buy new DEC UNIX machines because (a) we already
have lots of Suns, (b) some experienced SunOS users don't feel quite at
home on Ultrix.  Lack of support for new features similar to those that
SunOS has (like shared libraries) only biased people further against the
DEC machines.

Our system administrators are willing to support UNIX boxes from multiple
vendors, but they try hard to limit the number of operating
systems/architectures that they have to support.  Running 4.4BSD (or Mach
or GNU or OSF/1) on every system would be a big win, but running Ultrix on
some DECstations and OSF/1 on others would be a lose -- far better to run
the same O.S. on both kinds of systems.  If 4.4BSD were available for
enough platforms, it might become the O.S. of choice -- after which vendors
would be required to supply 4.4BSD-compatible hardware.  But 4.4BSD isn't
out yet, and I'm not holding my breath.

> most important: how much money did you give dec field service last year?  how
> much will you be giving them this year if you keep all your dec hardware?

We pay little or nothing in maintenance contracts for either hardware or
software (at least on our UNIX boxes--VMS is another story).  DECs interest
in supporting their old systems would be in trying to get us to buy new
systems.

Disclaimer: I'm not speaking for UT, and I'm not offically a sysadmin
anymore, so this is entirely my own opinion.

