Tech (VPI&SU - Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State University).  We
are a loyal customer of DEC's, with systems purchased for over a decade,
and with DEC as our preferred workstation supplier.  This is of particular
significance today since we have placed an order this spring for over $300K
in equipment, will be ordering almost as much in the upcoming months, and
have a very important student requirement as well.  In particular, around
100 students this fall, and a similar number in Fall 1993, will honor our
department's requirement for freshmen to buy a Personal DECstation.

We have heard in several news stories that DEC may not support OSF/1 for
DECstations as was previously promised.  As chair of our department's
Computing Resources Commitee, which plans and supervises our hardware and
software activities, this concerns me greatly, as it does the rest of CRC.
We have also heard comments from other faculty and students.  Of special
concern is the timing for incoming freshmen, who will be buying DECstations
soon, and who will certainly be confused if not upset by these discussions.

Therefore, I ask for clarification of DEC's position on this matter, and
pass on our suggestions.  We would certainly like to see an official letter
indicating DEC's software plans for the DECstations that we now have, have
ordered, or may be ordering in the near future.

While we understand your interest in Alpha, and look forward to being among
the earliest to get Alpha systems, we feel that support of DECstations is
also crucial.  Consider the freshmen of Fall 1993, who may use DECstations
for 5 years!  It certainly would be sensible for them to expect to use
OSF/1, not Ultrix, so they will be able to get continued support and 3rd
party products.  Similar arguments hold for machines in faculty offices
(like the one this message is being sent from), in research groups, and in
laboratories.

I look forward to your comments.  Feel free to pass this letter along to
others.  If you can tell me who to write, I will also send a version of
this in the U.S. Post.

Thank you for your time and assistance.

Sincerely,

Edward A.Fox
Associate Professor

