First off, let me mention that I'm very glad that DEC has come to its  
senses on this issue.  I'd also like to thank Gail Grant for keeping us  
informed and involving us in the process.  That said, I have a couple of  
comments:

In article <1992Jun9.204942.14081@PA.dec.com> grant@glg.ucs.dec.com (Gail  
L. Grant) writes:
> glad you are happy about it.  yes, it is very, very real.  did the other 
> computer company say publicly that they were evaluating whether to
> put OSF/1 on "certain Motorola machines" and then give you a chance to
> give them feedback on their evaluation?  well, DEC did.  direct
> customer quotes, gathered from both sales, my CABs and the
> newsgroups, were used in the decision-making process. 
> 
> as i mentioned in this newsgroup several months ago: things in DEC are
> changing. we asked, you answered, we responded.  sounds good to me. ;-)
>  before, you had to wait to tell it to your sales rep.  now you can say
> it directly on the  newsgroups.  it isn't that we don't listen to other
> sources(yes, there ARE customers who don't read newsgroups), but we
> DO listen to this source too.  
> 

The first paragraph is a bit murky to me, but it seems that the
implication is that DEC asked us whether or not we wanted OSF/1 on
MIPS, and then based on a careful evaluation of customer input, 
decided to offer it.  That's pretty clearly not true; DEC stated that
they would *not* offer OSF/1 on MIPS platforms.  Here are a couple of
paragraphs from Mitch Wagner (Senior Editor at Open Systems Today)
that were posted on May 30:  

> David Stone said the decision had been made not to port OSF/1 to the
> DEC line. He then said that the decision was subject to review based
> on customer input. I asked him if I should say this was a "tentative"
> decision then, he said, no, no, it was much stronger than
> "tentative"---but it was subject to review, still.
> 
> (We pause for a word now from our sponsor, Webster's Ninth New
> Collegiate Dictionary: "tentative: .... 1. Not fully worked out or
> developed.... ")
> 
> He also spent a good deal of time talking about how this is not the
> old DEC that changes its mind a lot about strategy. The decision has
> been reached, he said. It's final. It's definite. And its subject to
> review based on customer input.

I'm very glad that DEC has flip-flopped yet again on this issue, but
they have lost a great deal of credibility in my eyes.  Personally, I
don't plan to order anything from them again until they actually have
delivered a working, stable version of OSF/1 for the MIPS-based line
of DECstations.    

(As always, I speak only for myself, and you shouldn't confuse me
shooting my mouth off with official opinions of the University of
Michigan.) 

