In article <6869@public.BTR.COM>, thad@public.BTR.COM (Thaddeus P. Floryan) writes:
> In article <Bp8n19.BHw@world.std.com> cmr@world.std.com (Charles M Richmond) writes:

[Various claims about the joys of various other vendors and the problems
with VMS, DEC, its alleged lack of standards, etc. have been deleted.]

(First, just a footnote about QIC.  Realize that QIC on Sun and on HP are
very different.  Not important to the overall argument, just a note.)

As to whether VMS is open, I believe I have a lot of experience that helps
put this in perspective:

  1. My firm supports the same product (same sources) on most popular
     Unix workstations (e.g. SPARC, RS/6000, DG Aviion, HP 9000, DECstation),
     a number of less popular ones, and VAX/VMS and VMS POSIX.  This product
     is X Windows and Motif based.

  2. We are business partners with all the vendors mentioned, so we
     have a lot of experience with each of them and their products.  

  3. I personally have over 25 years in the industry and have been active on
     ANSI technical committees over 15 years.


Now for a couple of observations:

Software developed for the X Windows/Motif environment is extremely portable,
in general, and VMS is at least as easy a target as most Unix systems.  With
the POSIX option, you can completely ignore the traditional VMS DCL interface
and pretend that you are running "Unix" and it works.  Believe it.  I am not
saying that things couldn't be better, only that they are as good as they are
on any of the Unix platforms.  I will be glad to discuss details of what 
true "open" applications mean in a client/server world with anyone who wants
to via email.

DEC has probably the strongest internal commitment to standards of any vendor
I have worked with.  This was not always the case, but over the last 10 years
or so, they have worked diligently to develop and implement standards -- true
standards of recognized bodies (ANSI, ISO,...) and many of the de facto ones
in the marketplace.  

Since our products work the same in numerous environments, we try to stay out
of the "X (which I use) is great and Y (which you use) is garbage" flames.
Still, I have to defend VMS as being "open".  You lose little, if any, of your
Unix functionality when you go to VMS/POSIX.  You lose an enormous amount when
you go the other way.

(I don't want to hear about all those wonderful program development tools on
your favorite Unix system unless you have used some of the VMS ones like the
Symbolic Debugger and the Language Sensitive Editor, not to mention compilers
that can optomize without royally screwing up.)

I am not arguing that you should be using VMS, only that it certainly has a
valid claim to being "open".

BTW, it appears that the conversion of most applications from VAX/VMS to 
Alpha/VMS (or MIPS Ultrix to Alpha OSF/1) will likely be simpler that that
for Sun Solaris 1.0 to Solaris 2.0.

 


