Hello Nethackers,
we have a Sun under SunOS4.1.2 and a VAX running VMS 5.5 and
UCX 1.3. With many problems we now managed to export a VAX-VMS
directory and access it on our sun via NFS. 

Unfortunately, the mount requires superuser privileges on SUN-OS
(this is not the case on our Ultrix DECstations). UCX for VAX-VMS
requires UID/GID specification. The manual says, that the Superuser
is not allowed to appear in the UID/GID field of the UCX-proxy
specification.

Nevertheless we tried
     VAX> UCX ADD PROXY /UID=0/GID=230/HOST=*
     VAX> UCX ADD PROXY /UID=-2/GID=-2/HOST=*
     VAX> UCX ADD PROXY /UID=1002/GID=230/HOST=*

and (how ulgy, what about security?) 

     # chmod u+s /usr/etc/mount /usr/etc/umount

The first two UCX-lines seem to be necessary to be able to
mount the VAX-Directory. Because s-uid on mount User 1002
can mount the VAX-Directory and work with it, but it's
extremely ugly and probably contains security holes.

So, does SunOS allow mount for normal users (this would also
be fine for /pcfs) or can we overcome the problem on the VAX
using wild cards for uid/gid.

Thanks in advance
