From: woodford@fourier.ece.cmu.edu (Paul Woodford)
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
Subject: Alpha vs. MIPS performance
Message-ID: <1992Jun4.144532.24119@fs7.ece.cmu.edu>
Date: 4 Jun 92 14:45:32 GMT
Sender: news@fs7.ece.cmu.edu (USENET News System)
Organization: Electrical and Computer Engineering, Carnegie Mellon
Lines: 31

The SGI Crimson is based on the 50MHz/100MHz R4000.  It has a SPECint92 of
61.7 and a SPECfp92 of 63.4.  If we assume these figures increase linearly
with speed, a machine designed around the 75MHz/150MHz R4000 will have a
SPECint92 of about 93 and a SPECfp92 of around 95.

DEC claims that the initial implementation of Alpha will perform around 140
SPECmarks at 200MHz.  However, a system which needs to feed a 200MHz
processor is going to cost a lot more that a system which needs to feed a
75MHz processor.  So let's say that a comparable Alpha implementation runs
at 150MHz; assuming linear scaling, that gives 105 SPECmarks - not much more
than the R4000 at 75MHz/150MHz.

I know I'm ignoring a bunch of issues here - linear scaling probably won't
hold, cache sizes may change, etc.  There is no SPECmark92, so DEC's
marketroid figure is probably SPECmark89, which is artificially inflated
compared to '92 figures.  DEC isn't even supposed to talk about SPEC figures
until they can show the hardware and software used, or at least what they
assumed in their simulations.

It seems that the R4000 may have been unjustly maligned.  SGI has been
producing an R4000 system which is competitive on the SPEC benchmark with
similar HP and IBM systems since March (February?).  It's not all it could
be to floating-point dweebs like myself, but it's no slouch.  How can SGI
ship a whole new system when DEC can't even ship new daughter cards?

Paul Woodford
woodford@ece.cmu.edu

ps:  The Crimson's SPECint92 is the highest of any workstation, regardless
of price.  I'm posting SPECint92 and SPECfp92 figures from a table in
Microprocessor Report to comp.benchmarks


