In article <1992Jun4.144532.24119@fs7.ece.cmu.edu> woodford@fourier.ece.cmu.edu (Paul Woodford) writes:
>DEC claims that the initial implementation of Alpha will perform around 140
>SPECmarks at 200MHz.

Digital has not released any official SPEC numbers for any Alpha *system*.
Some of our marketing people are throwing around numbers, but you will
notice that there is an uncomfortable span in the numbers quoted. The
primary reason is that the final numbers will hinge more on the
compilers than anything else and they are improving/changing as we speak.

> However, a system which needs to feed a 200MHz
>processor is going to cost a lot more that a system which needs to feed a
>75MHz processor.
 
Why ? Both the R4000 and the 21064 use internal caches and run the external
bus and cache at some fixed fraction of the internal clocks, hence the
cost of the overall CPU support is not that different (the R4000 has 
a slight edge here needing a few less external parts, but that's maybe $20).

> So let's say that a comparable Alpha implementation runs
>at 150MHz; assuming linear scaling, that gives 105 SPECmarks - not much more
>than the R4000 at 75MHz/150MHz.

Do not do any linear scaling. It's going to be wrong because memory system
don't scale linearly. Otherwise you *may* be right, or not. Wait until
we can officially measure a number.

