Lines: 32

>     The hats have to go to Future Domian, they had one of the most
> compliant CAM implementation out there first.  The SCSI people from
> Ballard Synergy had a very early CAM version out there but unfortunately
> they didn't track the various CAM revisions very closely toward the end.

Rather than trying to track all the various revisions in the CAM spec
as it evolved, Ballard Synergy concentrated on drivers for many
operating systems (DOS, OS/2, Netware, Unix) for the various NCR
host adaptors.  I believe (I'm just an engineer here, so I can't say
with certainty, of course) that this is what customers are most
interested in.  If they are given a DOS SCSI disk driver and a DOS
SyTos tape driver, for example, they don't really care if the
drivers internally use CAM or ASPI or whatever.

Given this, it made sense to freeze on an early CAM release that
did everything our drivers needed, rather than going through a
new release cycle every couple of months to track the latest
CAM changes -- especially when it was not clear what CAM changes
would really survive into the final spec.

That doesn't mean we don't have or are not working on software that
implements all of the current CAM spec (well, those parts of the
CAM spec that don't contradict other parts of the CAM spec, anyway!).
Now that the CAM spec appears to be nearly done (assuming someone
cleans up the contradictions...), I predict that there will be more
full complinat CAM implementations from the usual vendors.

(Then there will come the fun part -- finding out where the CAM spec
was ambiguous and different vendors interpreted that ambiguity in
different ways...that will be interesting).

--Tim Smith


