>
>And only 4Mb to MOP boot from an INFOserver?

Because the VXT creates a "swap" partition on the Infoserver, and starts
paging.

The VXT's are pretty marginal X terminals, as X terminals go.

From my notes so far: (VXT 1.0 software)

1)  It would be pretty easy to get your configuration set up such that you
have the terminal configuration locked to the point where you can't change
it, except from another VXT.  Then you have to get on the infoserver
and remove one of the configuration partitions.

I would hope there's another way, maybe with the 1.1 software.

2)  There doesn't appear to be anyway to connect to the infoserver and 
run the configuration utility from my workstation.  So now I have to have
a VXT handy just to configure other VXT's.  It sucks.

I realize that the configuration software is in the X terminal, but I'd
like to be able to run it from this real nice workstation that DEC
sold me.  Last thing I need is another screen hanging around my
desk.

3)  The 19" monitors are some cheapo kind, (Panasonic?) and not the
Sony's or something nice like that.  I guess they're not bad, they're
just not great.

4)  I'm having trouble with my infoserver 150 crashing under any kind
of load.  Then  the terminals lose access to the paging partition and
don't recover after the infoserver boots.\

5)  Locking the the "Print Screen" selection doesn't gray out the\
menu selection.

6)  Locking all of the stuff under the Communication menu doesn't
grey out the "Communication" selection, it still will pop up the submenu, 
and shows all of it's selections greyed out.  More of an aesthetics thing
I guess.

7)  I still can't get the HP VUE environment to work with the VXT's.
The local window manager seems to screw things up.  Clients work fine, 
just not the environment as a whole.

8)  Speed doesn't seem to great.  With a 12 MB VXT2000, popping up
menus from the terminal manager window is slow.  Seems like 1-2 seconds.
Nowhere near the speed of the HP Xterminal that I have, or even the
NCD's.




All in all, I'm disappointed.  You would think that with all the really
great X terminals out there, NCD, HP, and others, and the junk that
DEC made previously (VT1300, 1200), that when bringing out a whole new
line, they'd be nothing short of phenomenal.

*sigh*
