robin@lsl.co.uk (Robin Fairbairns) writes:

>Was there _really_ no hair-tearing and baring of (male) breasts when the 
>Sun decision was announced?  (I didn't deal with Suns then, so I wouldn't 
>know...)

	It wasn't too bad where I worked when the SPARC came out. We had
a pile of Sun3s and a few 386is at that time. Sun basically gave us a
"trade in" on the Sun3s where we got a certain credit for each Sun3 against
a SPARCstation, and got to keep the Sun3. This was a fine solution for us,
since it's not as if the Sun3s suddenly stopped working, or anything.
The 386is there was a little bellyaching about, but we found a use for
them (there's always SOMEONE who really really wants that DOS stuff).

	It's been my observation that there are 2 general approaches
to hardware upgrades 
	Approach 'a': Across the board. Keep the same release of the
		same operating system across more or less the same
		hardware.
	Approach 'b': Trickle down. As newer better hardware comes
		in, the older hardware trickles down.
	Other: Stuck. This is not really an approach, it's a whole
		different problem. (see below)


Trickle Down
	I've seen a lot of academic and research sites (like the one I used
to work for) that follow approach 'b' - when the new machines show up, the
old ones migrate to student labs, staff homes, secretaries' desks, etc, etc.
The net effect of the "trickle down" approach is that it broadens your
computing base pretty smoothly, if you've got a reasonably competent
user base and a do-it-yourself attitude towards software.


Across the Board
	Trickle down clearly is a problem for customers that are trying
to maintain a standard platform production type environment. My guess is
that these users scream loudly whenever *ANYTHING* changes, be it a
change in hardware, software, be it from Sun, DEC, or HP.


Stuck
	The "Stuck" users are the folks who have a single critical
application, and no use for their computer whatsoever outside of
running that application. To them, "obsolete" box is literally junk.
User needs in this case (luckily) don't have a lot to do with current
hardware - the application is either "fast enough" so nothing needs to
change or it's "too slow" and they have to look at replacing the box.


	In my opinion (as someone who has managed systems for longer
than he wants to contemplate) the "standard platform" model is a basic
loss from the word 'go' - it's not realistic and it's almost always more
expensive, since you have to constantly assess the cost of either staying
put or making a quantum leap. Even Digital (an organization that uses a
number of computers in our daily work) follows the trickle down model
pretty well. My group's secretary uses a MicroVAXII, I have a 5500 file
server for the group, a DECStation3100 on my desk, my internet gateway is
a DECStation2100, we have a 5000's for the folks who need to run MOTIF ;)
write code and do multimedia stuff, and we'll probably start seeing Alphas
crop up and hopefully someday trickle down. The point is that we have
a totally mixed environment, running a wide variety of operating system
releases and hardware, and it all interoperates fine. There's even a
few machines running VMS and talking IP and some of our DECstations
talk DECnet and it all works together. Here's an amusing thought: just
to keep DEC's computing base working, DEC is going to have to keep
supporting ULTRIX and MIPS and VAXes for a long time to come....

	So, what's the point of this somewhat rambling discussion? I'm
not sure. Where I used to work, we didn't scream when Sun obsoleted our
whole installed base out from under us - we bought more. We grumbled a
bit over the 386is but decided we were stupid to have bought them in the
first place. ;)

	I shouldn't say anything about the DEC/MIPS/OSF/Alpha upgrade
scenario other than that it's complicated and a lot of us are committed
to making it work.

mjr.


