In article <1992Jun1.180858.1@amherst.edu>, jwmanly@amherst.edu writes:
 
> TP: Normally with 10-BASE-T twisted-pair Ethernet, two or more nodes
> are  connected together in a star configuration with some kind of
> repeater or concentrator at the hub.  But if you have only two
> stations, is it possible to wire them together (either normally or
> with some kind of special cable) and get them to talk to each other
> without a hub?  Or is the hub serving some logically important
> function?

Boy! Is that a loaded question. The answer to both parts is "Yes". Two 10baseT
stations may be directly connected. The hub performs very important functions
but those are related to retiming and collision detection/handling. Since
niether retiming nor collisions are relevent to a two station hookup, there is
no need for a hub.

Don't forget that you need to connect pair 2 to pair three (null modem).

