In article <1992Jun1.220150.5980@mintaka.lcs.mit.edu> I asked for help
with a 3100 dying with an exception (apparently an unaligned access)
when I tried to boot it via MOP.

The solution is to do an "init" before trying to boot via mop.  For
some reason, after a halt (or a failed boot, for that matter) the
machine is left in some inconsistent state which boot (surprisingly)
isn't overwriting when it downloads the boot code.

Many thanks to the following, most of whom suggested using init:

	Sharon Taylor <wensel@cory.berkeley.edu>
	olle@bellatrix.tdb.uu.se (Olle Eriksson)
	Roger Ivie <IVIE@cc.usu.edu>
	Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz <neideck@nestvx.enet.dec.com>
	smith@clion.shr.dec.com (Joe Smith)
	frandsen@jrfvax.enet.dec.com (John R. Frandsen)

If anyone is interested, I finally broke down and poked at DMS enough
to learn how to have diskless clients a la Sun, with everybody sharing
a single /usr partition, and the backup generic client (used for upgrades
and diagnostics) taking only 5 meg or so of disk when not in use.

-- 
