In article <1992May26.230550.11461@PA.dec.com>, mogul@pa.dec.com (Jeffrey Mogul) writes:
> When George Carette first wrote the crashme program, his theory was
> that RISC designers cut corners.  He managed to prove his theory was
> wrong (find a list of which machines crash and which don't, and see
> if the RISC machines are noticably worse).

The thing to do is to go back to early releases of say the VAX 780
with VMS 2.0 and compare that with say with SUN OS 4.1.1 on a Sparc.

Run crashme on both systems and see what happens.

Or, take an equivalent system right off the shelf, version 1.0
of the system with the current operating system.

I compared for example a VAXSTATION with a SPARCTATION. Both
machines being released at approximately the same time, and
both machines requiring "new device drivers" and "new minor versions
of the OS" in order to be supported.

It is pretty damned obvious that it is fair to say that somebody
cut some corners. In engineering, in testing, *someplace*.

For example, let us investigate the notion that operating system X
is superior to operating system Y because it is

 "it is easier to bring up X on new hardware"

or

 "X came up faster"

The things that "came up faster" will generally have a more difficult
time with crashme. However, "time to market" is an important
"performance" factor too.

The hardware and the software have to be designed together and
both have to be working extremely well before it is worth wasting your
time trying to use them.


-gjc

p.s.

Actually, I'd me more interested to hear what Gordon Bell said about
crashme.


