Hi, all.  I have a question about disks and about 10-BASE-T.

DISKS: We at Amherst are about to purchase seven large disks to attach
to our VAX 8550 via a 5 Mbit CMD-1000 BI-to-SCSI controller card. Our
choices for the disks have basically narrowed to two: The DEC RZ58 or
the Hitachi DK516C.  Both are 1.3 GB, though the RZ58s cost about 10%
more after all the discounts are added up.

So the question is, what do people know about the real performance of
these drives?  If I go with the RZ58s, am I really getting anything
for that 10%? I know that  the RZ58s run at the new 5400 RPM speed,
while the DK516Cs only do 3600, and that the RZ58s do SCSI-2 (not
certain yet about the DK516Cs), but I don't know what that actually
means in terms of performance, nor do I know anything about the
longevity and MTBFs of this equipment.  Has anyone done any
benchmarking or used both themselves?

TP: Normally with 10-BASE-T twisted-pair Ethernet, two or more nodes
are  connected together in a star configuration with some kind of
repeater or concentrator at the hub.  But if you have only two
stations, is it possible to wire them together (either normally or
with some kind of special cable) and get them to talk to each other
without a hub?  Or is the hub serving some logically important
function?

