Well, let's see.

   1)	Sun chose to adopt the SPARC architecture and stick with it.
	They made that clear to all their customers, who went ahead
	and made long-term plans knowing that Sun was pigheaded enough
	to follow through with a mediocre architecture rather than
	flip-flop on customers.  The installed customer base and the
	rather "strong" belief of Sun users in the product made it
	possible.

   2)	Sun made the decision early on to design SPARC in-house.  DEC
	may have made a similar decision at the same time, but the
	inertia of the corporation made it impossible for DEC to
	release ALPHA in time to acquire market share; DEC will be
	18 months behind HP in releasing a RISC processor, which at
	least had only one committment to one processor type before
	that time (680*0).  Alternately, DEC managers may have been
	stupid enough to watch all other major workstation players
	begin RISC efforts, while they sat and twiddled their heads
	together.

   3)	When DEC decided to go with ALPHA, they knew obliteration
	would be their fate unless they offered a RISC workstation
	for the short-term.  In comes MIPS, the byte-ordering thing
	is carried over to a new architecture, and a pizza box is
	born.

   4)	DEC does not know how to market workstations.  To make that
	evident, they utterly botched the industrial design at a time
	when ergonomics were becoming more important.  This was on
	top of generally mediocre hardware and a slew of temporary
	and intermediate solutions.  Then some moron comes along and
	decides to call something TurboChannel.  Hey, DEC, turbocharging
	is usually reserved for gasoline and diesel engines.  Too bad
	they didn't rename their mouse into The Hockey Puck Locator.

   5)	DEC does not know how to design workstation software.  Even
	today, DEC's offerings are laughable in the best of times.
	It all seems to have been written in some alternate universe,
	or at least it may have been excellent there but somehow
	suffered in the transition.

   6)	DEC either outright lied to its developers and customers,
	or just basically blew it, or both.  This made it more difficult
	for everyone to make any careful long-term planning using
	DEC hardware and software.

All of this couldn't happen at a worse time for DEC, because the
mainframe and mini markets are on their way to oblivion.  Their
workstation sales are probably close to ceasing altogether as
customers jump the MIPS platform faster than hockey pucks off a
sinkining ship.  That means less cash and more bleeding.  A lost
workstation market share is very difficult to recoup, especially
after you've demonstrated your committment to blundering at every
turn.  Furthermore, the price/performance ratio is simply not going
to sway the entire universe of workstation users, or a large portion
thereof, into buying DEC ALPHA workstations.  There are organizational
issues, software issues, compatibility issues, design issues.

DEC has by now managed to:

    -	Miss the window for an internally-developed RISC architecture
    -	Carry the VAX workstation line way too long
    -	Carry VMS way too long
    -	Avoid adding sound, video, etc. to the base hardware
    -	Committ to everything and nothing all the time and none of the time
    -	Design mediocre hardware and software
    -	Lose too much money for a major computer player in 1991-92
    -	Hire the public relations department from hell
    -	Hire the software development department from hell
    -	Hire middle management from hell
    -	Claim heaven is right around the corner
    -	Make many customers and developers think of divorce

It doesn't look very rosy to me, ALPHA or no ALPHA.  This humpty
is slowly dumpting, and all the king's MIS departments and all the
king's network solutions couldn't put DECty together very well again.



